ath11k(4) bring-up tools
========================

ath11k-debug.sh
---------------

Loads if_ath11k with the debug knobs below set as tunables, waits for the
wlan device, scans (and with -a associates using /etc/wpa_supplicant.conf
and runs dhclient), and packs logs, PCI and MSI state, interrupt counts and
firmware files into /var/tmp/ath11k-debug-<date>.tar.gz.

Before running it:
  - pkg install wifi-firmware-ath11k-kmod-wcn6855_hw20 (or fwget(8)); the
    WCN6855 hw2.1 chips load the same files;
  - kern.msgbufsize="4194304" in loader.conf, so nothing scrolls away;
  - dumpdev="AUTO" in rc.conf, so a panic leaves a crash dump;
  - do not load if_ath11k at boot for the first runs; run the script from a
    console or a wired link.

Knobs it sets (all can be set by hand too):
  compat.linuxkpi.ath11k_core_debug_mask=0x1276  ath11k WMI HTC MAC BOOT
                                                 QMI REG PCI messages
  compat.linuxkpi.mhi_debug=1       MHI core debug messages
  compat.linuxkpi.qrtr_debug=1      every QRTR packet and QMI message
  compat.linuxkpi.80211.debug=0x13005b  LinuxKPI 802.11 traces
  sysctl compat.linuxkpi.mhi_dump=1 prints the MHI state of every controller

Reading the output
------------------

The boot goes, in order (summary.txt keeps the matching lines):

 1. "MSI vectors: 32" from ath11k.  A warning about falling back to one
    vector means MSI allocation failed; nothing after that can work.
 2. "after request_irq msi_addr ... msi_ep_base_data ..." (PCI debug): the
    address must not be 0; it is what the firmware writes interrupts to.
 3. MHI: "Requested to power ON", firmware image loaded ("successfully
    loaded firmware image" from LinuxKPI, or "Error loading firmware"),
    BHI, READY, M0 in SBL, BHIe, then mission mode.  On failure "mhi: state
    dump" shows the device registers (MHISTATUS state in bits 15:8, READY
    bit 0, SYSERR bit 2; BHI_EXECENV 0 PBL 1 SBL 2 AMSS; BHI_STATUS bits
    31:30 2 success 3 error; BHI_ERRCODE) and, per event ring, the device
    and host read pointers.  A device read pointer ahead of the host's
    means the device posted events the host never processed: its
    interrupt did not arrive (compare vmstat-i-before/-after).
 4. QRTR: "endpoint ... up on mhi0_IPCR", "hello from node N", "new server
    service 0x45 instance ...", and the lookup "looks up service 0x45
    instance ...".  "no lookup matches" means ath11k asked for another QMI
    instance than the firmware announces.
 5. QMI (QMI debug and qrtr_debug): host capability, memory request and
    ready, board data and M3 download, FW ready.  "response ... for no
    transaction" means a reply came after its waiter timed out.
 6. WMI/HTC up, then the wlan device and scan results.

mhi_sim
-------

A model of the device side of an ath11k-class MHI device, a test-only
kernel module that runs the MHI host core, the MHI bus glue, QRTR and QMI
without hardware:

	make -C mhi_sim && kldload linuxkpi_mhi ./mhi_sim/mhi_sim.ko
	sysctl debug.mhisim.run=1; sysctl debug.mhisim.result

debug.mhisim.inject=1 makes the device fail the BHI download, =2 drops the
event ring interrupts, to see the failure reports.

rht_test
--------

A test-only kernel module for the LinuxKPI rhashtable that ath11k keeps its
peers in.  Load it and read the result from dmesg:

	make -C rht_test && kldload ./rht_test/rht_test.ko && kldunload rht_test
	dmesg | grep rht_test
